.TH ARES_SET_QUERY_QUEUE_OPTIONS 3 "24 September 2026"
.SH NAME
ares_set_query_queue_options, ares_get_query_queue_options \- configure outgoing
query scheduling and merging
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ares.h>

ares_status_t ares_set_query_queue_options(ares_channel_t *channel,
                                            size_t max_active,
                                            size_t max_pending,
                                            ares_bool_t coalesce);

ares_status_t ares_get_query_queue_options(const ares_channel_t *channel,
                                            size_t *max_active,
                                            size_t *max_pending,
                                            ares_bool_t *coalesce);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions configure optional transaction scheduling on an initialized
channel. By default, both limits are zero and merging is disabled, preserving
immediate query dispatch.
.PP
A positive \fImax_active\fP limits simultaneous network transactions, including
internal server recovery probes. Additional distinct transactions wait in FIFO
order, up to \fImax_pending\fP. Retries retain the original transaction's active
slot. A waiting transaction begins its network timeout only when transmitted;
these limits do not impose a maximum wall-clock wait in the queue.
.PP
The sum of the limits must not exceed 32768. A zero \fImax_active\fP requires a
zero \fImax_pending\fP and disables the active limit. If merging alone is enabled,
at most 32768 distinct transactions can be outstanding. A full queue rejects a
new distinct transaction with \fBARES_EQUEUEFULL\fP through its callback and the
status-returning query API. Matching followers and completed-cache hits do not
consume another transaction slot. This is not a bound on total caller memory:
each accepted follower still needs callback state.
.PP
With \fIcoalesce\fP set to \fBARES_TRUE\fP, identical active or waiting requests
share one network transaction and its response. Equality compares the complete
serialized DNS query and internal send policy, ignoring only the transaction ID.
Name case, flags, EDNS data and record order are preserved. Changes to routing
configuration exclude existing transactions from subsequent merging. Explicit
server recovery probes are never merged with user requests.
.PP
Merging is independent of the completed-response cache. A follower joins the
remaining lifetime of the existing transaction without restarting its deadline
or retry budget. Its timeout count includes only timeouts observed after it
joined. Each caller retains its own completion callback and retry disposition.
.PP
The optional query ID output is populated at admission with the reserved ID that
will be used on the wire, even for waiting requests. Followers share that actual
wire ID. Waiting reservations are not eligible to receive DNS responses until
transmitted. Cache hits keep the existing synchronous completion behavior and do
not allocate a transaction ID.
.PP
Callbacks can run before a query API returns. Completion claims each caller
immediately before invoking its callback. If a callback calls \fBares_cancel(3)\fP,
remaining unclaimed followers are cancelled along with other outstanding callers.
Queries created by cancellation callbacks survive the outer cancellation, unless
a nested cancellation explicitly cancels them. Channel destruction completes
outstanding callers without promoting waiting work; it retains the restrictions
documented by \fBares_destroy(3)\fP.
.PP
Configuration can only change while the channel has no outstanding callers and
is not dispatching a completion or updating its server list. \fBares_dup(3)\fP
copies this configuration but
does not copy outstanding work. The older \fBares_save_options(3)\fP interface
does not represent these settings; use the getter to retrieve them separately.
The getter requires all three output pointers.
.PP
\fBares_queue_active_queries(3)\fP counts logical callers, including waiting and
merged callers, plus ungrouped internal queries. \fBares_queue_wait_empty(3)\fP
waits for all such callers to finish.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.B ARES_SUCCESS
The configuration was set or retrieved.
.TP
.B ARES_EBADQUERY
A required pointer is NULL, a limit combination is invalid, or the boolean
setting is invalid.
.TP
.B ARES_EBUSY
Outstanding work, completion dispatch or a server-list update prevents
reconfiguration.
.TP
.B ARES_ENOMEM
Allocation of scheduling state failed. Existing configuration is preserved.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ares_send (3),
.BR ares_query (3),
.BR ares_queue (3),
.BR ares_cancel (3),
.BR ares_destroy (3),
.BR ares_dup (3)
